  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Malagasy names are long compounds with a meaning, often a blessing or a wish. Highland names commonly start with the prefix Ra- (a mark of respect) or Andria- (once a noble prefix, now common), as in Rakotomalala or Andrianjafy. Traditionally every child got a name of their own rather than inheriting one, but today many families pass the father's name down as a surname. On forms the long name is written first in capitals, but day to day people go by a short Malagasy given name (Hery, Fanja, Tiana) or a French Christian first name (Jean, Marie). Rakoto and Rasoa are the everyday stand-ins for any man and woman, like John and Jane. Coastal peoples (Betsimisaraka, Sakalava, Antandroy and others) have their own naming styles, often without the Ra- prefix; they are not yet listed here.",
